course: dictionary
lesson: 12175
title: Short and clear: concise
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Concise means short and clear. It says what is needed in few words, without leaving out anything important. When you meet a new word, look at the words around it for clues. «Please give a concise answer to the question.» A concise answer is short, but it still answers the question.
explain: «Write a concise summary of the story in three sentences.» The clue is in the words three sentences. A summary is a short version, and concise tells you to keep it short and clear, with the main points and nothing extra.
explain: Concise is not the same as too short. «The map was concise and showed only the main roads.» Nothing important is missing. The opposite of concise is wordy or long-winded: «His answer was wordy and went round in circles before it said anything.» Ask yourself: could I say this in fewer words and still keep every important point?
fact: Concise means short and clear, saying what is needed in few words.
fact: A concise answer does not leave out important things, even though it is short.
fact: «A concise dictionary» is shortened but still covers the main points.
fact: The opposite of concise is wordy or long-winded.
fact: In science, a concise conclusion states the result simply.
exercise: «Give a concise answer.» What does concise mean here? | Written in pencil | *Short and clear | Long and detailed | Loud and bold
exercise: «His reply was wordy and long-winded.» What is the opposite of wordy? | Funny | Whispered | *Concise
exercise: «The teacher asked for a concise summary of the story.» What should a concise summary have? | *The main points in few words | Pictures and drawings only | A long list of jokes | Every single word of the story
test: «The science report had a concise conclusion: the plant grew taller in the sunlight.» What does concise conclusion mean? | A conclusion written in a different language | A conclusion with no result | A long conclusion full of extra detail | *A short, clear conclusion that states the result simply
test: Ruby says her answer was concise, but it was only one word and missed the main point. Is she right? | Yes, one word is always concise | No, concise means long and detailed | *No, concise answers are short but still include what is important | Yes, because long answers are never good
exam: Which sentence uses concise correctly? | «She wrote a concise note and then forgot to write the time.» | *«She wrote a concise note: meeting at 3 o'clock, bring your book.» | «She wrote a concise note in a very loud voice.» | «She wrote a concise note that went on for ten pages about nothing.»
